Resource

A transfer of more data than one packet holds, over an established
link. Seven context bytes, six of them link data packets and one a
proof. Start at doc/link for the link itself and at doc/fields for the
field names.


Constants

    MAPHASH_LEN         4   bytes     RNS/Resource.py#MAPHASH_LEN
    RANDOM_HASH_SIZE    4   bytes     RNS/Resource.py#RANDOM_HASH_SIZE
    SDU                 464 bytes     RNS/Resource.py#SDU
    HASHMAP_MAX_LEN     74  entries   RNS/Resource.py#HASHMAP_MAX_LEN
    WINDOW              4   parts     RNS/Resource.py#WINDOW

HASHMAP_MAX_LEN is derived from the link MDU and is how many four-byte
map hashes fit in one advertisement or one update. It decides where a
transfer needs a second segment, and nothing on the wire states it.


Contexts

    0x01   resource        one part
    0x02   resource_adv    an advertisement, msgpack
    0x03   resource_req    a part request
    0x04   resource_hmu    a hashmap update, msgpack
    0x05   resource_prf    a proof, and a packet of type proof
    0x06   resource_icl    the sender cancels
    0x07   resource_rcl    the receiver cancels

    RNS/Packet.py#RESOURCE


One token, cut into parts

The data is encrypted once, through the link, and the resulting token
is then cut into parts of SDU bytes:

    self.data = self.link.encrypt(self.data)

    RNS/Resource.py#link.encrypt

So a part is a slice of one token and not a token of its own. Only the
first part begins with the initialisation vector and only the last ends
with the hmac, and no part can be opened alone. A decoder that reads a
part as a token finds 16 bytes it takes for an iv and no hmac that
verifies.

The packet layer does not encrypt a part either, for the same reason it
does not encrypt a keepalive: the payload is already ciphertext.

    RNS/Packet.py#RESOURCE

A part is named by its map hash, four bytes:

    map_hash = SHA-256(part || random_hash)[0:4]

    RNS/Resource.py#get_map_hash

The random hash is the resource's, from the advertisement, so a part
packet does not carry enough to name itself. That is not an oversight:
the receiver matches parts against a hashmap it already has, and a part
that arrives without one belongs to no transfer it is expecting. No
vector records a map hash for that reason.

Four bytes is short enough to collide, and the reference says so: it
keeps a guard list while cutting and remaps the whole resource if two
parts hash alike.

    RNS/Resource.py#collision_guard_list


Advertisement, 0x02

A msgpack map of eleven one-character keys, in this order:

    t   uint   transfer size, the encrypted length
    d   uint   data size, before compression and encryption
    n   uint   number of parts
    h   bin    resource hash, 32 bytes
    r   bin    random hash, 4 bytes
    o   bin    original hash, 32 bytes; the first segment's
    i   uint   segment index
    l   uint   total segments
    q   bin    request id, or nil
    f   uint   flags, one byte
    m   bin    hashmap, four bytes per part

    RNS/Resource.py#dictionary

The order is the order the reference writes them and msgpack preserves
it, but a map is not a sequence: a reader that goes by position rather
than by key agrees with this reference and with nothing that is
entitled to reorder them. unpack names all eleven and names them in
another order than pack writes them, so neither the order nor the count
is a rule of the format.

    RNS/Resource.py#unpack

test/linkdata/resource-advertisement-reordered is that map: the same
eleven keys and the same values, written by the reference's own
umsgpack in the order unpack reads them. It is the same length as the
advertisement it was built from and not the same bytes, and the
reference reads the two identically.

What follows from reading by key is that a key which is not there costs
its own field and nothing else, and that a key which is there and is
none of the eleven costs nothing at all. Four vectors carry the cases,
all of them plaintexts the layers above the packet never build:

    resource-advertisement-no-hash        h is absent; the other ten
                                          fields all read
    resource-advertisement-not-a-number   t is a string; the ten keys
                                          after it still read
    resource-advertisement-not-a-map      one byte, no map at all;
                                          eleven dashes
    resource-advertisement-extra-key      a twelfth key, second in the
                                          map; the eleven still read

The last is the direction a decoder is likeliest to get wrong, because
nothing the reference writes today exercises it. The key is two bytes
where every name in the format is one, and its value is a float64 where
the format holds only uints and bins, so a reader that expects the
shape it has seen rather than the shape msgpack declares stops at it
and loses the ten keys behind it. The reference does not read the map
at all until umsgpack has turned it into a dictionary, and then asks
that dictionary for eleven names.

A uint is written at the width the value needs and a bin at the width
its length needs, so the widths in an advertisement are decided by the
size of the resource. Every one the format can reach is in
test/linkdata/resource-advertisement-large, a resource of 100000
bytes:

    t, d      uint32, 0xce, above 65535
    n         uint8,  0xcc, 216 parts
    m         bin16,  0xc5, a hashmap of 296 bytes

Every other advertisement here holds those as a fixint, a uint16 and a
bin8. A decoder that reads the width it saw last, or that implements
one branch per key, passes them and fails on the first resource that
is not small.

    RNS/vendor/umsgpack.py#_pack2

The resource hash is over the data and the random hash together, so two
transfers of identical data are two resources:

    hash = SHA-256(data || random_hash)

    RNS/Resource.py#random_hash

The flags byte is six bits, low to high:

    0   encrypted
    1   compressed
    2   split
    3   is request
    4   is response
    5   has metadata

    RNS/Resource.py#self.f

Only the byte is recorded. Two of the six bits have a vector, and each
comes with an object that sets it rather than from a table of
permutations; see doc/packet. Bit 0 is set in every transfer here,
because a resource on a link is encrypted. Bit 5 is set in
test/linkdata/resource-advertisement-metadata, whose resource was
constructed with metadata: the map is the same 11 keys and nothing but
this byte says the metadata is there.

The hashmap in an advertisement holds at most HASHMAP_MAX_LEN entries,
which is 74 at this MTU. A resource with more parts than that
advertises the first segment and sends the rest later, which is what
0x04 is for. test/linkdata/resource-advertisement-large is such an
advertisement: it declares 216 parts and carries 74 hashes, and the
count and the length of the hashmap are the only two places that say
so.


Part request, 0x03

Three pieces concatenated, with no count, no type byte and no
delimiter. The reader takes them by length:

    offset  size  field
    0       1     hashmap exhausted: 0xff, and 0x00 otherwise
    1       4     the last map hash, only when exhausted
    -       32    the resource hash
    -       -     four bytes per part requested

    RNS/Resource.py#request_data

The flag is one value compared against HASHMAP_IS_EXHAUSTED, not a
byte tested for truth. A sender writes only 0x00 or 0xff, and a reader
that treats every non-zero byte as exhausted looks for the last map
hash where a 0x01 has no last map hash to find.

    RNS/Resource.py#HASHMAP_IS_EXHAUSTED

The flag decides where the resource hash begins. A decoder that reads a
fixed offset is right on every request until the receiver runs out of
hashes, and then reads the resource hash four bytes early and every map
hash after it wrongly.

The receiver asks for at most WINDOW parts at a time, and the window
grows as parts arrive, so the number of map hashes in a request is not
fixed either.

Nothing in the payload states its own length, so the plaintext is the
only bound a reader has. The least a request can be is one byte, and
once the flag has been read the least it can be is

    33 bytes    the flag and the resource hash
    37 bytes    when the flag is set, with the last map hash between

A plaintext shorter than that is rejected as short-plaintext, with the
threshold beside it; see doc/fields. The packet around it decoded, so
the header, the token and the keys stand. Four vectors hold the four
ways to be too short:

    test/linkdata/resource-request-empty
    test/linkdata/resource-request-short
    test/linkdata/resource-request-exhausted-short
    test/linkdata/resource-hashmap-update-short


Hashmap update, 0x04

    offset  size  field
    0       32    the resource hash
    32      -     msgpack [segment index, hashmap]

    RNS/Resource.py#hmu

Sent by the sender when a part request says the receiver has run out of
hashes. The hash is raw and what follows it is msgpack, which is the
one payload in the corpus that is half of each.

The msgpack half carries its own lengths and a reader cannot run past
them. The raw half carries none, so the least an update can be is 33
bytes: the hash and one byte of the array behind it. Shorter is
rejected as short-plaintext.

That one byte does not have to be an array header, and a byte that is
not one is no rejection either. The rule is the one a request body
follows: the elements it would have yielded are "-", the hash before it
is fixed width and reads either way.
test/linkdata/resource-hashmap-update-not-an-array is 33 bytes whose
last is 0x01. See doc/link.


Proof, 0x05

A packet of type proof, not data, and not encrypted, addressed to the
link id. Its payload is 64 bytes:

    offset  size  field
    0       32    the resource hash
    32      32    SHA-256(data || resource_hash)

    RNS/Resource.py#proof_data

That is the same packet type and the same length as an implicit
delivery proof, and neither half means here what it means there: the
second 32 bytes are a hash of the data, not a signature over anything,
and nothing about the packet is signed. The context byte is the only
thing that tells the two apart, which is why the corpus files this as
its own kind, resourceproof, rather than as a form of proof.

A receiver that has the whole resource can compute it. So can anyone
else who has the data, which is what it is for: it says the transfer
arrived, not who sent it.

64 bytes is the only length that decodes. Any other is rejected
as invalid-length, with the one accepted length beside it, and
test/resourceproof/invalid-length is one byte under. A delivery proof
has two accepted lengths where this has one, and 64 is one of them: a
decoder that reads the context byte and then hands the packet to its
delivery proof path takes a hash of the data for a signature.


Cancels, 0x06 and 0x07

The resource hash isi 32 bytes. 0x06 is the sender giving up and 0x07 the
receiver. Either end sends one only while it still believes the transfer
is running: a resource that has completed cancels nothing.

    RNS/Resource.py#COMPLETE


Vector format

kind: linkdata for six of the seven, determinism: encode

raw is three lines, as for every other packet on a link:

    line 1    the link request packet
    line 2    the responder's 32-byte link private key
    line 3    the complete packet

    test/linkdata/resource-advertisement
    test/linkdata/resource-part
    test/linkdata/resource-request
    test/linkdata/resource-request-exhausted
    test/linkdata/resource-hashmap-update
    test/linkdata/resource-cancel
    test/linkdata/resource-cancel-receiver

The exhausted request and the hashmap update come from a transfer of
more parts than one hashmap segment holds; the others from a transfer
of four parts, and the two cancels from one that is stopped after the
advertisement.

kind: resourceproof, determinism: encode

raw is two lines:

    line 1    the resource hash the receiver was advertised
    line 2    the complete packet

The hash is an input because the packet names a resource and says
nothing else about it. hash_match is the comparison, and it is the only
check a resource proof admits without the data.
